WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.290
We used to think that the brain

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:06.900
controlled all our movements,

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.210
so when you had a spinal cord injury,

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.330
if you lost that communication
between the brain

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.650
and the spinal cord, there's
really nothing you can do

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.670
unless you regenerate that connection.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:19.800
It turns out, the spinal
cord has much more

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.910
of a sophisticated role
than we thought before.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:26.830
We can stimulate the neural
networks in the spinal cord.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:30.040
We can help the spinal cord relearn

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.690
how to do things it was able
to do before the injury.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.150
I was supposed to have been an individual

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.170
that was gonna live and
die from this chair.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.600
I mean, with the epidural stimulator,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.263
it's so close to the point
of just moving this leg.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.283
You're growing the ability to recovery.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.660
I really think that it's gonna give me

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.760
the availability to get out of this chair.

00:01:01.871 --> 00:01:03.900
Growing up, we started out with go karts,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.260
and then we got into
bicycles and everything.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.150
My grandpa took me to my
first Supercross race.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.530
Seeing that, it instilled
me with this want

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.670
that that's what I wanted to do.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.820
My career was at the point
where I was amateur pro.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.340
It was a couple races, and
then I had my pro points,

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.190
where then I could race the
stuff that you see on TV.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.190
I went out and I was
testin' a new motorcycle

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:25.190
that I had just got.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.070
And it was like a hundred foot jump.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:29.490
Right off the face of that jump,

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.860
I remember the motor just locked up.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.977
It had me pinned, and then it just ,

00:01:34.977 --> 00:01:37.780
and just tumble, tumble, tumblin'.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.560
Some people came up to me
and were like, you okay?

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.440
And I'm like, no, I don't think so.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.273
Can you get up?

00:01:42.273 --> 00:01:43.106
I was like, no.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.470
You're paralyzed from the waist down.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:48.660
You're a complete injury.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:49.493
You can't feel anything.

00:01:49.493 --> 00:01:51.880
You're not gonna regain
any mobility, any function.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.350
From here on out you're gonna
have to have daily care,

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.210
more than likely.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.560
You're gonna live from a wheelchair.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.033
That was a lot to swallow.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.640
Basically how I took it was
that from that point on,

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.280
my life was starting to end,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.550
versus it continuing to be an adventure.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:08.900
That was how it felt to me.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.480
When I got that prognosis,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.030
the first thing I went
to was looking at centers

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.520
that offered rehab and locomotor training,

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.130
and which were the top ones.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:22.870
That was where I kinda
heard through the grapevine

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:24.540
that they had a guy in Louisville

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.160
that was going through the procedure

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.970
for the epidural stimulator,

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.763
and they were seeing some very
interesting things happen.

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:34.542
Left toe up.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.460
We were doing a series of experiment.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.203
We implanted the individual
with the epidural stimulator.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.880
He was laying down and he, on his own,

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.460
tried to move voluntarily.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.247
He tried to move his toes,
and he wiggled his toe.

00:02:56.247 --> 00:02:59.275
And I said, holy S***.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.280
We did not anticipate that.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.400
We tested it, the stimulator
off, and they cannot move.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.250
When you turn the stimulator on,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.130
they're able to move.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.170
This dude's got a switch
that just makes him normal.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.500
That's how it was making me perceive it.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.880
Our whole theory about how

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.163
the motor system works was challenged.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.000
So the traditional way of thinking

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.670
about a spinal cord
injury is that the brain

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:37.480
is the primary controller
of movement and function,

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.160
but it turns out the spinal circuitry

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.310
is as smart as the brain.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.890
It can learn, it can
remember, it can forget,

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.203
and it can make decisions.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.790
How we actually walk is this
circuitry already has in it

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.853
the ability to generate patterns to walk.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.830
As devastating as a spinal cord injury is,

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:03.530
that circuitry still has
the ability to do that,

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:07.513
but it loses what we would
call state of excitability.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.880
So the epidural stimulator is

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.780
an off-the-shelf pain stimulator

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:15.850
that's used in people for pain.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:20.410
It has a lead that's placed
in the lower spinal cord,

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.260
and it generates an electric field.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:27.050
So the stimulator actually
helps those neurons

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.230
that are still viable,
that are still alive,

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.430
to be able to function
like they did before.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:48.210
It's five days a week, two sessions a day.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.820
I started with a toe.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.170
And then it started with an ankle,

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:53.160
and then it started with a leg.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.420
12 sessions in I got my first holt

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.450
of independence in standing.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:58.520
It's gotten to the point now

00:04:58.520 --> 00:04:59.880
where it just happens naturally.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.550
I just lean forward on my walker

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.990
and think that I wanna stand, and I stand,

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:05.583
and my legs, they hold me.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.380
When you immediately turn it on,

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:10.710
you don't really notice, but
once you get a couple volts up,

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.250
it's like you feel the
body charged and ready.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.750
When you're trying, you feel
that go through your head,

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.243
from your brain, down
your spine, to that leg.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.980
Give 'em some body weight
support, keep them balanced,

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:27.580
and then people move their
legs in a walking-like pattern.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.570
That sends the right
information about walking

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:33.280
back to the spinal cord,
which it's expecting.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:34.870
And because the spinal cord's smart

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:39.350
and it can learn, it starts
relearning how to walk again

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:43.140
with that information and the
person thinking about walking.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.400
And over time, in some people,

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.290
they're able to step independently,

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:51.290
and in two people, walk
completely independently

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:52.790
from physical assistance,

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:56.623
but just use a walker or
canes to walk over ground.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.340
The biggest thing is feeling it,

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.950
because after injury, you feel nothing.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:08.540
And when you execute those
movements, you feel them.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:10.200
It's gonna be a really quick move.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.950
Shift, and then load.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:12.783
There you go.

00:06:12.783 --> 00:06:15.160
I exploded a vertebrae.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.090
What is known before all this

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.600
is there's no way, no
how that I'd ever be able

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.970
to take a step again, but that's wrong.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:24.800
The next step really
needs to be to design one

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.023
that is very specific for the shoes.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.260
So the device that we envision
is akin to a smartphone.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.410
It can be voice activated.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:34.680
Says, okay, I wanna stand now.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.050
So you have your stand program.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.470
I wanna walk now, you
have your walk program.

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.130
It has closed loop feedback.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:41.550
The blood pressure is monitored.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.783
Do all of this as
autonomously as possible.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:49.240
This is not a cure.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.600
There's not going to be
a silver bullet for this.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:56.080
There's not anywhere, I
think in the near horizon

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.400
where we're gonna completely regenerate

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.610
the spinal cord after an injury.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:04.930
But if we can make an
incremental change in function,

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.043
it improves their participation in life.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:09.430
One of the things that hits you

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:10.880
when you're in the hospital,

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:12.280
am I ever gonna get married?

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.500
Am I gonna have kids?

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.693
And at this point, I'm married.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:18.700
And the epidural stimulator program

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.010
gave me the ability to
stand at my wedding.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:22.860
As soon as she started
walking down the aisle,

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.504
the emotion just hit me.

00:07:24.504 --> 00:07:27.627
I don't really cry,
but man, I was ballin'.

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:34.890
Even getting to dance with my mom.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.363
That was so awesome.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:42.400
And I feel so close to the kids thing.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:44.247
I know it's comin'.

